WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.480
Object lifetime is the portion of execution during which an object exists and can be used according to its type and storage rules.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.100
Lifetime is not the same as the continued existence of a numeric address.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:19.840
A pointer value can be copied, stored, logged, or passed around after the object it once designated no longer exists.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.620
Pointer validity is therefore a live condition.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.800
A pointer is safe to use only while it designates a valid object for the intended access.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:33.620
Dangling pointers occur when code keeps or uses a pointer after the referenced object's lifetime has ended.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:40.640
Returning a pointer to an automatic local object is unsafe because that object's lifetime ends when the function returns.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:47.260
Ownership is the practical engineering rule that says which code is responsible for preserving or releasing a resource or object.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.680
Borrowed pointers should not outlive the object they refer to.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:57.780
Ownership transfer should be explicit in function contracts so callers know whether they must free, retain, or stop using a pointer.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.560
Cleanup paths are part of lifetime design.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:10.380
Error handling, early returns, partial initialization, and shutdown code should make it clear when an object's lifetime ends and which pointers must no longer be used.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:16.600
If multiple pointers designate related storage, cleanup should also make the aliasing relationship clear to reviewers.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.740
The safest C interfaces communicate lifetime expectations directly.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:28.080
They document whether a pointer is borrowed, owned, retained, optional, mutable, read-only, or valid only until the next call.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.300
That documentation keeps memory safety from depending on hidden conventions.
